// States that exist and do not decide.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What this file is
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Until now a war could end two ways: the loser was eaten, or the loser was let go. There
// was no third, and the third is most of history. A protectorate, a puppet, a dominion, a
// satellite - a country that keeps its army, its economy and its flag, and answers to
// somebody else about what it does with them.
//
// Each kind below is a row. Add a row and the game has a new kind; nothing in the code
// names any of these, and `SubjectCatalog` reads whatever is here.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What a row means
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//     tribute            share of the subject's weekly tax that goes to the overlord
//     manpower           share of the subject's manpower growth that goes with it
//     joins_wars         does it turn up when the overlord is attacked
//     dragged_into_wars  does it turn up when the overlord ATTACKS somebody
//     may_declare_war    may it start a war of its own
//     overlord_defends   does the OVERLORD come when the subject is attacked
//     liberty_desire     how much it wants out, before circumstances are counted
//     grace_days         how long after being made a subject before it starts wanting out
//     demand_cost        what imposing it costs at a peace conference, in war score
//     annex_after_days   how long before the overlord may absorb it outright, -1 never
//     annex_below_liberty  and how contented it has to be to go quietly
//
// `liberty_desire` is the base, not the answer. What a subject actually feels is worked out
// every week from its size against its overlord's, how stable the overlord is, and whether
// the overlord is losing a war - because a dependency is a bargain about power, and it is
// renegotiated the moment the power changes.
{
  "weights": {
    // How much a subject's own size counts. A dependency the size of its overlord is not a
    // dependency for long, and history is short of exceptions.
    "size_pull": 0.55,

    // An overlord who cannot govern itself cannot govern anybody else.
    "overlord_instability_pull": 0.40,

    // A war the overlord is losing is the moment every subject has ever chosen.
    "overlord_at_war_pull": 0.20,

    // And the other direction: overwhelming strength is its own argument.
    "overlord_strength_hold": 0.35,

    // What liberty desire has to reach before the subject simply leaves.
    "break_at": 1.0,

    // How fast it moves. Per week, so a subject that has decided takes a year or two to
    // get there rather than announcing it the following Tuesday.
    "drift_per_week": 0.02,

    // How large a subject an overlord will absorb without asking, against its own size. A
    // small province quietly becomes part of the country holding it; a dependency a third
    // its size is a country, and swallowing one is a decision rather than a formality.
    "annex_size_limit": 0.25
  },

  "protectorate": {
